## Overview

This program performs a parallel mergesort on an array of random integers using pthreads. It accepts 
three parameters: input size, cutoff level (maximum recursion depth at which new threads are spawned), 
and the random seed for the array.

## Manifest

- mergesort.c: Parallel mergesort implementation (merge, my_mergesort, parallel_mergesort, buildArgs)
- mergesort.h: Declarations and globals (A, B, cutoff) and struct argument
- test-mergesort.c: Test driver (CLI parsing, data generation, timing, correctness check)
- Makefile: Build rules for objects, dependencies, and executable

## Building the project

Prerequisites:
- gcc and make
- pthreads (on Linux/WSL this is provided by default; link flag is handled by the Makefile)

Build (WSL/Linux/macOS):
make

Rebuild from scratch:
make clean && make

Result:
- Executable: test-mergesort

Windows notes:
- If building outside WSL, use an environment that provides gcc and make (e.g., MSYS2/MinGW).
- The output may be test-mergesort.exe and can be run as:
.\test-mergesort.exe <input size> <cutoff level> <seed>

## Features and usage

Features:
- Parallel mergesort using pthreads with a configurable cutoff (threading depth)
- Deterministic input generation via RNG seed
- Timing output for the full sort
- Post-sort correctness check (verifies array is nondecreasing)

Usage:
./test-mergesort <input size> <cutoff level> <seed>

Arguments:
- input size: Number of array elements to be sorted (e.g., 1000000)
- cutoff level: Maximum recursion depth that spawns new threads (0 = single-threaded)
- seed: Random seed used for array generation

Examples:
./test-mergesort 1000000 0 1234    # single-threaded baseline
./test-mergesort 1000000 2 1234    # spawn threads up to depth 2
./test-mergesort 1000000 4 42      # deeper parallelism with a different seed

Output:
- On success: prints elapsed time in seconds, e.g., "Sorting N elements took X.XX seconds."
- On failure: prints an error and exits if the array is not sorted

## Testing

To test my program, we primarily ran the provided test-mergesort executable multiple times with 
different cutoff values to ensure the program completed without crashing. We focused on fixing runtime 
errors such as segmentation faults and double-free issues during execution. Once these issues were 
all resolved, we submitted the files to the gradescope page, which further tested the implementation, 
and with the first submission everything came back successful.

## Reflection and Self Assessment

During testing and development we ran into a few segmentation fault errors which stemmed from 
incorrectly implementing memcpy and attempting to free the args ourselves before realising that 
the test-mergesort.c handled this. When attempting to test the program using make, we received a 
console error which after deliberation with other teams was fixed by running make again. 

We expected more issues/errors to arise due to multiprogramming with pthreads, however we ran into 
little trouble. Completing the program definitely helped to contextualise the functionality of 
threading and how they speed up a program such as this one. 
